From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-agent: num_sta follows the station list

993 was meant to stop a lying HAL from zeroing the client count, and it did
not work on hardware. Measured 2026-08-27 on an agent serving two clients,
in a settled state well after boot:

	iw dev ap-mld-1 station dump           2 stations
	ubus wifi.ap.ap-mld-1 assoclist        both clients returned
	ubus wifi.ap.ap-mld-1 status           "num_stations": 0
	ubus map.agent status, fronthaul BSS   "num_sta": 0, stations: [2 entries]

A BSS holding two stations in its own list while reporting none beside them
is not a HAL problem any more - the two fields come from the same struct in
the same dump (agent.c, "num_sta" and "stations" in the same table).

The reason 993 could not help is that its guard sits in parse_ap(), and
parse_ap() has exactly one user: agent_init_interfaces(). It runs once, at
init - and stations are only added to ap->stalist afterwards, by
agent_init_interfaces_post_actions(). So list_empty(&ap->stalist) is always
true when the guard runs, the first branch always wins, and the branch that
counts is dead code.

At runtime the two-stage async refresh owns this data, and neither stage
writes the count:

	stage 1  bss_status_data_cb     channel, bssid, ssid, utilization,
	                                max_stations - num_stations is not
	                                even in the policy
	stage 2  bss_stations_data_cb   maintains ap->stalist: adds via
	                                wifi_add_sta(), releases after
	                                STA_ABSENT_MAX missing replies

That leaves the association events (agent.c: num_sta++ / num_sta--) as the
only writer of a value the refresh path keeps rebuilding beside them. One
missed event and the count is wrong until the daemon restarts, because
nothing reconciles it.

It also explains an oddity that looked like the HAL being selectively
honest: on the same node the backhaul BSS reported num_sta 2 correctly while
the fronthaul reported 0, though the HAL answered "num_stations": 0 for
both. The backhaul number came from events that did fire. Nothing about the
HAL differed.

So count the list where the list is maintained. After the sweep, so a
released station is already gone, and in the same block that just decided
who is present. The count is then derived from the entries by construction
and the two can no longer disagree - which is what 993 set out to do.

The guard in parse_ap() is left in place. It is harmless, and it is correct
for the case it describes should that function ever be reused at runtime.

Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
---
--- a/src/agent.c
+++ b/src/agent.c
@@ -3029,6 +3029,7 @@
 	 * momentarily empty reply does not drop live clients. */
 	{
 		struct sta *s, *tmp;
+		uint32_t n = 0;
 
 		list_for_each_entry_safe(s, tmp, &ap->stalist, list) {
 			if (s->absent < STA_ABSENT_MAX)
@@ -3038,6 +3039,22 @@
 			    __func__, ap->ifname, MAC2STR(s->macaddr), s->absent);
 			wifi_del_sta(w->a, ap->ifname, s->macaddr);
 		}
+
+		/* The list above is the only runtime authority on who is
+		 * associated to this BSS, so derive the count from it here
+		 * rather than leave the two to drift. Nothing else recomputes
+		 * ap->num_sta on this path: stage 1 (bss_status_data_cb) does
+		 * not even parse num_stations, and the guard added by 993 sits
+		 * in parse_ap(), which runs only from agent_init_interfaces()
+		 * - at that moment stalist is still empty, so its else branch
+		 * never executes. That left the association events as the sole
+		 * writer, and a missed event desynchronised the counter for
+		 * good. Counted after the sweep, so a released station is
+		 * already gone. */
+		list_for_each_entry(s, &ap->stalist, list)
+			n++;
+
+		ap->num_sta = n;
 	}
 
 	w->parsed = true;
